    "tarball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/Yoruiopz/Codex-Blender-Bridge/tarball/v0.3.0",
    "zipball_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/Yoruiopz/Codex-Blender-Bridge/zipball/v0.3.0",
    "body": "# Blender Codex Bridge 0.3.0 \u2014 faster structured scene work\n\n108 MCP tools, including 20 additions for more precise, lower-round-trip Blender editing.\nThis remains **alpha software**: use backups and verify edits before saving important work.\n\n## Download and install\n\nDownload the assets attached to this release, not GitHub's automatic source archives:\n\n| Asset | Install |\n| --- | --- |\n| `blender_codex_bridge-0.3.0.zip` | Blender \u2192 Edit \u2192 Preferences \u2192 Add-ons \u2192 Install from Disk; select the ZIP without extracting |\n| `blender_codex_bridge-0.3.0-py3-none-any.whl` | In your standalone Python 3.10+ environment: `python -m pip install ./blender_codex_bridge-0.3.0-py3-none-any.whl` |\n| `blender-codex-bridge-plugin-0.3.0.zip` | Optional Codex plugin with MCP configuration and Blender Studio guidance; requires the installed MCP command on the client's PATH |\n| `SHA256SUMS.txt` | SHA-256 checksums for the three packages |\n\nBlender 4.2+ is the declared minimum. Real-Blender tests use 4.5.1 LTS and 5.1.2.\nPip needs internet to install dependencies. No source build is required.\n\nEnable the add-on, open **N \u2192 Codex Bridge**, review permissions, and start the listener.\nRegister the MCP executable with\n`codex mcp add blender_codex_bridge -- \"<absolute path to blender-codex-mcp executable>\"`.\nUse manual registration **or** plugin registration, not both. Restart Codex/start a new\ntask and begin by asking for bridge status and scene inspection without edits.\n\nSee the [complete installation guide](https://github.com/Yoruiopz/Codex-Blender-Bridge/blob/v0.3.0/README.md#install--no-source-build-needed).\nThe repository remains private; downloads require repository access.\n\n## New capabilities\n\n- **Batching (2 tools):** preflight and execute 1\u201332 ordered structured steps, bounded results,\n  per-step permission checks/history, cooperative cancellation between steps, explicit partial-failure evidence.\n- **Interaction (4 tools):** explicit object selection/active object, supported mode transitions,\n  paginated mesh-component inspection and indexed vertex/edge/face selection with stale-context checks.\n- **Layout (4 tools):** filtered scene queries, up to 128 object transforms, world-origin alignment\n  and distribution with measured post-state and conservative dependency checks.\n- **Geometry Nodes (10 tools):** create/inspect node trees, add interface sockets and reviewed nodes,\n  set properties/defaults, edit links, remove nodes, and attach graphs as modifiers.\n- **MCP compatibility:** structured success and error envelopes work with SDK 2.0.0 and 2.2.0;\n  actionable permission and disabled-tool errors remain visible without exposing internal tracebacks.\n- **Release reliability:** matching component versions, wheel metadata/runtime checks, deterministic\n  ZIP packaging, checksums, generated README inventory, and installable CI development artifacts.\n\nAll prior modeling, materials/shader nodes, UV, modifier, constraint, rigging, animation,\nscene, camera/light/world, render, inspection, status/history, checkpoint and save tools remain.\nThe [README](https://github.com/Yoruiopz/Codex-Blender-Bridge/blob/v0.3.0/README.md#features)\nlists every tool and the [workflow guide](https://github.com/Yoruiopz/Codex-Blender-Bridge/blob/v0.3.0/docs/agent-workflows.md)\nexplains limits and examples.\n\n## Upgrade from 0.2.0 (or 0.1.0)\n\n1. Save/back up your work normally. Stop the bridge and finish active tool calls.\n2. Replace the old Blender add-on with the 0.3.0 ZIP and restart Blender to unload old modules.\n3. In the **same environment registered with Codex**, run:\n   `python -m pip install --upgrade ./blender_codex_bridge-0.3.0-py3-none-any.whl`.\n4. Remove the historical `mcp==2.0.0` constraint from your installation instructions/requirements;\n   0.3.0 no longer needs it. An already-installed SDK 2.0.0 remains compatible.\n5. Update the optional plugin if used. Restart Codex/start a new task and start the bridge.\n6. Verify add-on version with `bridge.status` and MCP version with\n   `python -m pip show blender-codex-bridge` in the registered environment; both should be 0.3.0.\n7. Review permissions, enable only required toolsets, and begin with inspection.\n\nDo not mix add-on/server versions. No `.blend` migration is required, and installation\ndoes not intentionally modify your scene. Selection IDs and undo checkpoints are session-local.\nRollback requires reinstalling both older packages; software downgrade does not undo scene edits.\n\n## Safety and known limits\n\n- Literal `127.0.0.1` only; never tunnel the listener. Blender checks permissions on its main thread.\n- Batch planning is not a scene simulation or complete handler-argument validation. Earlier successful\n  edits remain after a later failure; there is no automatic atomic rollback. Inspect before retrying.\n- Long Blender operations cannot be interrupted mid-call; stop/cancellation takes effect at safe points.\n- Layout uses origins, not surface spacing, and rejects dependent objects rather than guessing.\n- Geometry Nodes is an allowlisted subset, not all nodes/zones/bakes. Shared graph edits need acknowledgement.\n- Sculpt/paint mode entry is not brush automation; compositor, advanced NLA/driver/weight workflows,\n  simulation/bakes and other specialist workflows remain outside the structured surface.\n- Python stays disabled by default. It requires Python, delete, external-file and save permissions\n  together and is **not a sandbox**. Do not enable it merely to bypass a structured permission denial.\n- Checkpoints are global undo markers, not durable backups. Scene summaries/images go to the AI client;\n  local transport does not imply local-only model processing.\n\n## Verification\n\nRelease gates include the full non-Blender suite, lint and type checks; Python 3.10/3.11/3.12\nwith MCP SDK 2.0.0/2.2.0 in CI; clean wheel installation; and isolated factory-startup Blender\nchecks for baseline tools, interaction, layout, Geometry Nodes, and batching on 4.5.1 and 5.1.2.\nThese tests use temporary scenes, not the user's open project. They do not establish that every\nBlender version, editor or production workflow is supported.\n\n\nRelease source: `e1b0bbd4464682df3d93402747d8b0c44a464387`. Verified: 283 tests on both MCP SDK versions, ten Blender smoke runs, and [all seven CI jobs](https://github.com/Yoruiopz/Codex-Blender-Bridge/actions/runs/35770812050).\n"
  },
